| 6 | `python3 -m pytest scripts/sbom-survey/tests --tb=no -q -rsE` | `1` | `collected 22 items` → **`22 failed`** — 0 passed, 0 skipped, 0 errors |
| 7 | `git diff --name-only cbb56212` | `0` | no manifest, no lockfile, nothing under `src/`, `engine/`, `.github/` |
| 8 | `grep -n 'SCHEMA_VERSION' src/rust/crates/fathomdb-schema/src/lib.rs` | `0` | still `24` |

**The suite runs to completion: 22 collected, 22 FAILED, 0 passed, 0 skipped, 0 errors.** pytest
prints only the non-zero counters, so the literal summary line is `22 failed in 0.20s`; the zeroes
are established by `collected 22 items` (nothing errored during collection), by `22 failed` equalling
the collected count (nothing passed), and by `-rsE` emitting **no** skipped and **no** error section.

That property is structural, not incidental. The `require()` helper in
`scripts/sbom-survey/tests/conftest.py` does the `sbom_survey` import **inside the test body** and
converts a missing module into a `pytest.fail` naming the criterion and restating the required
behaviour; `run_cli()` does the same for the out-of-process CLI criteria. A module-level
`import sbom_survey` would abort collection and hide 21 of the 22 criteria; a `pytest.skip` would be
a vacuous green. Neither appears anywhere in the suite.

**Red for the right reason** — the two failure classes §5 items 3 and 4 turn on:

