   202	`pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`, `requirements*.txt`, `setup.py`. `yarn.lock`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`,
   203	`poetry.lock`, `Pipfile`, `setup.cfg` are in the recognized table but currently match nothing —
   204	they are present so that adding one to the repo is *discovered* (and then fails REQ-4 tiering
   205	loudly) rather than silently ignored.
   206	
   207	### 5.2 Fixture manifests — excluded by a data rule, and audibly so
   208	
   209	`dev/release/fixtures/cargo-skew/**` (4 × `Cargo.toml`) and `dev/release/fixtures/pip-skew/**`
   210	(4 × `setup.py`) are **deliberately fake, deliberately skewed** manifests that exist to make the
   211	release version-skew gates demonstrate their catch. A naive "everything tracked" scope emits them as
   212	real dependencies, and every downstream consumer then sees phantom packages at wrong versions.
   213	
   214	**Decision.** They are **excluded from the BOM**, by an `action = "exclude"` rule in the tracked
   215	`tiers.toml`, with `reason = "fixture"` — and the excluded set is **carried in the survey output**
   216	(`survey.excluded`, mirrored into the CycloneDX `metadata.properties` as
   217	`fathomdb:excluded-manifest`) so the exclusion is auditable rather than invisible.
   218	
   219	**Why exclusion and not a fourth tier.** §3a ruled the tier vocabulary as
   220	`shipped` / `dev-tooling` / `eval-only`. Adding `fixture` would silently re-litigate a HITL ruling
   221	and, worse, would put non-existent packages in the component list where a vulnerability feed or a
   222	`purl` consumer would treat them as real. Exclusion-with-a-record is the honest shape: they are
   223	manifests, they are tracked, they are knowingly not dependencies.
   224	
   225	**Why data-driven.** The rule lives in `scripts/sbom-survey/tiers.toml` next to the tier rules, in
   226	the same first-class rule list, so:
   227	
   228	- it is reviewable in one file rather than grep-able out of a code path;
   229	- a future fixture directory is added by editing data, with no code change;
   230	- `AC-SBOM-06` can *prove* it is data-driven — the test loads a tier map with the rule removed and
   231	  asserts the fixture manifests then flow through (and trip REQ-4), which a hardcoded `if
   232	  path.startswith("dev/release/fixtures/")` could not satisfy.
   233	
   234	**Free consequence.** Because the only tracked `setup.py` files are the four excluded pip-skew
   235	fixtures, the tool needs **no `setup.py` execution or AST parsing** — the single most fragile parser
   236	in the Python packaging space is never written. If a real `setup.py` is ever tracked it is
   237	discovered, hits no tier rule, and fails loudly (REQ-4) rather than being mis-parsed.
   238	
