The slice is intentionally test/spec-only, but several acceptance tests do not enforce requirements stated in the accompanying spec. These gaps would allow an incomplete SBOM implementation to turn the suite green while missing recognized manifests, purls, or the transitive dependency graph.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Include setup.cfg in manifest expectations — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/slice-31-sbom-spec/scripts/sbom-survey/tests/conftest.py:39-51
  When a tracked `setup.cfg` is added, `tracked_manifest_paths()` will not include it even though the design explicitly lists `setup.cfg` as a recognized manifest type, so AC-SBOM-03 can still pass while the implementation silently misses that manifest. Please add `setup.cfg` to the recognized names used by the test helper so future Python config manifests are covered by the red/green oracle.

- [P2] Assert purls on CycloneDX components — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/slice-31-sbom-spec/scripts/sbom-survey/tests/test_cyclonedx.py:32-35
  For any implementation that emits schema-valid CycloneDX components with only `name`/`version`, this test still passes even though REQ-6 requires a `purl` per component and the design uses purls as stable component identity for advisory matching. Please add a per-component assertion that `purl` is present and has the expected ecosystem prefix.

- [P2] Make dependency graph coverage non-vacuous — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/slice-31-sbom-spec/scripts/sbom-survey/tests/test_cyclonedx.py:91-98
  On the current repo, an implementation that emits only direct components and omits all lockfile-derived library↔library edges can still pass here because `doc.get("dependencies", [])` may be empty and the only depth coverage assertion requires a `direct` component. That misses the core REQ-8/library-graph contract, so please assert the dependencies array is non-empty and that at least one known lockfile-only component is tagged `transitive`.
